acceptance:
Each project/store and validated run incarnation gets one host-persisted transition record. It holds:
- the frozen declaration: canonical owner, action and item;
- the declaration and result digests, as integrity fields only;
- the delivery state and the acknowledgement state;
- the correlated run wait and queued prompt.
Registration persists the record together with its pending run wait in one commit. Terminal settlement persists the queued prompt and consumes the wait in one commit. The record is kept after the wait is consumed.
A rescan creates nothing new after consumption, after a restart, or after the run directory disappears and reappears. A changed declaration or result digest blocks that same record. A run's first terminal result is not a conflict. Tests show each case.

A transition's resume prompt is promoted or sent only when all of these hold:
- the transition record (incarnation/version, owner, prompt id and content, hold nonce) has its exact durability proof;
- the queued prompt has its exact durability proof;
- the transition is still current.
It is never released after a failed commit. Every release path enforces this:
- the generic queue-durability acknowledgement;
- its Changed re-request;
- the rejected-prompt requeue;
- boot load before orphan recovery, which needs a validated durable transition, queue and wait-consumption snapshot, or else keeps the prompt held with an actionable reason.
Tests attempt each path with the transition proof missing and show zero promotions, then exactly one promotion once both proofs exist. They also show that a stale acknowledgement after Stop or Cancel releases nothing, and that a replacement prompt cannot inherit an old acknowledgement. Ordinary prompts behave as before.

Deterministic barrier tests use the test-only verified provenance and route input. They show exactly one logical sign-off and one resume prompt in each of these cases:
- the host is down before terminal indexing;
- a restart before the registration commit;
- a restart after registration;
- a restart after the terminal enqueue but before dispatch;
- the ambiguous send/receipt boundary, reconciled through the w-9b3a delivery receipt and never re-sent.
An owner that is stopped, paused, archived, deleted or unverified is never woken or substituted. Its transition shows an explicit Blocked or Cancelled disposition that a rescan does not undo. No polling, watchdog or timer is added.
